Airfoil Surrogate Modeling

Machine learning surrogate models for rapid prediction of airfoil aerodynamic coefficients (CL/CD) using CST parameterization. Achieves near-instantaneous predictions with less than 2 percent error within the design space.


Overview

Aerodynamic shape optimization is bottlenecked by the high computational cost of repetitive simulations. This project develops ML surrogate models that predict lift and drag coefficients 1000x faster than traditional CFD, enabling rapid design space exploration.

Key Features

- Instant Predictions – Millisecond inference time
- High Accuracy – Less than 5 percent error within design space
- Multiple Models – GPR, XGBoost, Random Forest
- Comprehensive Dataset – 2,233 samples, 7 AoAs
- CFD Validated – Compared against Ansys Fluent

---

Dataset

Design Space Generation

- Base Airfoils: NACA 0012, 2412, 4412, 63215-B, Clark Y
- Parameterization: CST with 12 shape weights (6 lower + 6 upper)
- Sampling: LHS with +/-15 percent perturbation per base airfoil
- Geometries: 65 per base family (5 x 65 = 325 total)

Aerodynamic Data

- Solver: XFOIL (viscous panel method)
- Reynolds Number: 500,000 (incompressible flow)
- Angles of Attack: -2, 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 degrees
- Total Samples: 2,233 converged cases

Feature Space

Inputs (13): 6 lower CST weights, 6 upper CST weights, Angle of Attack
Outputs (2): Lift Coefficient (CL), Drag Coefficient (CD)

---

Models

GPR - Gaussian Process - Uncertainty quantification, smooth interpolation
XGBoost - Gradient Boosting - Non-linear relationships, computational efficiency
Random Forest - Ensemble Bagging - Robustness, feature importance


---

Quick Start

Installation

git clone https://github.com/dalekennethsteve/Surrogate-Modelling-for-Airfoil-Performance.git
cd airfoil-surrogate

python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

pip install -r requirements.txt

Load a Pre-trained Model

import joblib
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

gpr_cl = joblib.load('models/gpr_cl.pkl')
scaler_X = joblib.load('models/scaler_X.pkl')

new_airfoil = [-0.15, -0.09, -0.10, -0.07, -0.08, -0.08,
               0.20, 0.21, 0.24, 0.16, 0.21, 0.19, 4.0]

columns = ['w_l1','w_l2','w_l3','w_l4','w_l5','w_l6',
           'w_u1','w_u2','w_u3','w_u4','w_u5','w_u6','Alpha']
data = pd.DataFrame([new_airfoil], columns=columns)
X_scaled = scaler_X.transform(data)

cl_pred = gpr_cl.predict(X_scaled)[0]
print(f"CL = {cl_pred:.4f}")

Train Your Own Model

import pandas as pd
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from xgboost import XGBRegressor

data = pd.read_csv('data/processed/dataset.csv')
X = data[['w_l1','w_l2','w_l3','w_l4','w_l5','w_l6',
          'w_u1','w_u2','w_u3','w_u4','w_u5','w_u6','Alpha']]
y = data['CL']

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)

model = XGBRegressor(n_estimators=450, max_depth=7, learning_rate=0.08)
model.fit(X_train, y_train)

print(f"R2 Score: {model.score(X_test, y_test):.4f}")

---

Results

Validation on Unseen Airfoil (NACA 2411)

AoA    GPR CL Error    GPR CD Error    XGBoost CL Error    XGBoost CD Error
4 deg  0.13%           0.60%           2.49%               2.01%
6 deg  1.72%           5.07%           4.49%               3.39%
8 deg  1.41%           0.01%           4.10%               2.75%
10 deg 0.85%           1.80%           3.91%               3.60%

Interpolation at 5 Degrees (Untrained Angle)

Airfoil    GPR CL Error    GPR CD Error
E22        0.65%           0.26%
G56        0.64%           0.23%
B64        3.09%           2.75%

Key Findings

- GPR achieves less than 3% CL error for most in-distribution cases
- Interpolation at untrained angles works exceptionally well
- XFOIL systematically underpredicts drag vs CFD (20-40% difference)

---

Validation Methods

Base Airfoil: Predict on parent airfoils excluded from training
Interpolation: Predict at 5 degrees AOA (not in training data)
Test Airfoils: Validate on unseen geometries from design space
CFD Comparison: Compare against Ansys Fluent SST k-omega simulations

